Why: turnstile opens an elogind session (service "turnstiled") for each
user's service manager, so every user service and D-Bus activated app maps
to that background session. polkit, GDM and GNOME expect systemd's
semantics, where user@.service processes belong to no session and fall back
to the user's display session; with the background session the polkit agent
is never found for them. Chimera patches one caller (SetBrightness) for the
same reason; this fixes the lookup for all of them.
Upstream: none (turnstile-specific; see chimera-linux/cports main/elogind/patches/turnstile-dbus-session.patch)
Drop when: turnstile stops registering the user manager as a session.

--- a/src/libelogind/sd-login/sd-login.c
+++ b/src/libelogind/sd-login/sd-login.c
@@ -46,7 +46,20 @@
         assert_return(session, -EINVAL);
 
         r = cg_pid_get_session(pid, session);
-        return IN_SET(r, -ENXIO, -ENOMEDIUM) ? -ENODATA : r;
+        if (IN_SET(r, -ENXIO, -ENOMEDIUM))
+                return -ENODATA;
+        if (r < 0)
+                return r;
+
+        /* A user service manager started by turnstile gets a session of its
+         * own. Like systemd's user@.service, treat its processes as outside
+         * any session, so callers fall back to the user's display session. */
+        _cleanup_free_ char *service = NULL;
+        if (sd_session_get_service(*session, &service) >= 0 && streq(service, "turnstiled")) {
+                *session = mfree(*session);
+                return -ENODATA;
+        }
+        return r;
 }
 
 _public_ int sd_pid_get_unit(pid_t pid, char **unit) {
